JJ wrote in David how should we evaluate  the economy it seems like I can always  find someone saying things are great and  someone else saying things are awful and  I don't know enough about economics to  know who's right do you have any advice  or recommendations for how to analyze  the economy and know when things are  doing well and more importantly how do  we know what's causing the economy to do  well or poorly how do we separate  correlation and causation Wow  these are very very good questions and  not questions with a simple answer I  think that the political underpinnings  of what you're saying is you will see  the stock market do well or not do well  and you'll see people say on that basis  that the economy is doing well or that  it's not doing well and then the second  layer of that is saying well even if the  stock market is doing well it's not  because of anything the President did or  even if the stock market is doing poorly  it's not because of anything the  President did if you want to defend a  president you can look at unemployment  rates you can look at job creation you  can look at inflation consumer  confidence index trade balance the  answer is sadly as with most areas of  economics there actually is no one  indicator that you can look at to  determine how the economy is doing the  stock market can give you a sense as to  the perceived value of investors of  companies that are publicly traded but  of course those values can become those  share prices can become overvalued they  can also reflect the deliberate sort of  ignoring of economic realities that are  going on in other parts of the economy  and remember the overwhelming majority  of stock ownership is highly highly  concentrated so whether the economy is  doing well or not at large  only slightly relates to how the stock  market is doing you can look at  unemployment for example problem with  unemployment is that number one when you  stop looking for work you're no longer  considered unemployed this is just a  reality has nothing to do with Democrats  or Republicans in the White House that's  how it's always been measured that is  often manipulated for political purposes  additionally the unemployment rate  doesn't tell you  what is happening with wages we've had a  very very long period of wage stagnation  in the United States you can take a look  at something like the Consumer Price  Index which tells us about inflation but  of course different levels of inflation  represent either a concern or a non  concern depending on what other factors  are going on so there's really no one  indicator you can look at and that's why  it really requires more in-depth  thinking you should consider  unemployment jobs created job quality  change in wages the impact of the stock  market on things like retirement  accounts because that dictates a lot of  whether people approaching retirement  age are able to afford to retire you can  look at things like trade balance Trump  doesn't really seem to understand it he  just throws out we lose on trade we went  on trade whatever there is really no one  indicator and there aren't even really  specific numbers it's not like well if  inflation is up 4% but the stock market  is up 10% then it's okay that there's  really just nothing like that and this  is something that really requires a lot  of thought and nuance and it's not  something you're really ever going to  get from corporate media sadly I think  house ownership rates is a good metric  though because that's really like a  middle-class metric it seems like the  American Dream here is to be able to  work hard enough so that one day you'll  be able to afford your own home but then  again I think a lot of people are living  in cities nowadays you see a lot more  renters they're really just is no one  perfect metric out I actually forgot to  mention homeownership rate but yeah and  homeownership rates can go up or down  not only based on real estate prices but  also based on mortgage rates which  connects to Federal Reserve policy so  again important but it doesn't tell us  everything that we need to know so I  think that the best thing I can tell you  is to learn about all of the different  indicators and really try to take a more  sort of holistic approach to analyzing  how the economy is doing you're not  going to get it from corporate media  that's for sure so you're going to need  to recur to other sources
 

